title: _3
---
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: ...
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: From now on, things will start getting... complex.
ORC: So far, you've seen that each instruction is executed only once.
ORC: From first to last, from top to bottom.
ORC: With the introduction of LOOPs, instructions may be executed forever, never, or a certain number of times.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: Preferably a certain number of times.
<<play 3 0>>
===

title: _3_0
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: LOOPs have conditions that can be true or false.
ORC: Just like the Goals you need to satisfy.
ORC: And a sequence of other operations that belong to that LOOP.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: They're basically the lines with dashes in front of them.
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: When the compiler reaches one, it checks whether the condition is true.
ORC: If it is, it enters its own block.
ORC: Otherwise, it skips the entire block and moves on.
ORC: Once inside, it executes the instructions in the block one by one, then returns to the LOOP instruction.
ORC: So, if it has become true, it goes back inside and starts over...
<<jump dom5>>
===

title: dom5
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Everything clear?
-> Please tell me how the loop works again.
    ORC: LOOPs have a condition that can be true or false.
    ORC: Just like the Goals you need to satisfy.
    ORC: And a sequence of other operations that belong to that LOOP.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: They're basically the lines with dashes in front of them.
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: When the compiler reaches one, it checks whether the condition is true.
    ORC: If it is, it enters its own block.
    ORC: Otherwise, it skips the entire block and moves on.
    ORC: Once inside, it executes the instructions in the block one by one, then returns to the LOOP instruction.
    ORC: So, if it has become true, it goes back inside and starts over...
    <<jump dom5>>
-> But if the code runs forever, do I pass the test or not?
    ORC: If it runs forever, your test is considered FAILED.
    ORC: Remember that it checks the same condition again on every cycle.
    ORC: And that condition depends on variables.
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: And you can change the value of variables with SET.
    <<jump dom5>>
-> Can I create a LOOP too?
    ORC: Yes, you can, and you will have to...
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: ...sooner or later.
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: It costs 4 energy, but if used well, it can save you much more than that.
    ORC: After creating one, all subsequent instructions will go into its block.
    ORC: Then, if you want to write more instructions outside it, you can use the <-- button.
    ORC: For now, though, I suggest you focus on learning how it works.
    <<jump dom5>>
-> Crystal clear
    <<show ORC2>>
    ORC: Let's hope so.
    <<show ORC1>>
    ORC: For now just click START and follow the flow.
===

title: _3_1
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Sometimes it's useful to control entry into and exit from a LOOP by changing its variables right under its nose.
ORC: Other times, it isn't.
ORC: Now let's look at more substantial LOOP blocks.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: Don't disappoint me.
===

title: _3_2
---
<<show ORC1>>
ORC: Choose the simplest path.
<<show ORC2>>
ORC: Don't make things harder for yourself.
